WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:03.960
Honduras. it's one of
the most dangerous places

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.540
in the world to be an
environmental activist.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.590
The case of Berta Cáceres is emblematic,

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.130
it's a microcosm of
the situation in Honduras

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:14.880
for many activists and defenders.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:18.760
Berta Cáceres,
one of the country's most well-known

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.520
indigenous environmental leaders,
was gunned down in her home

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.730
early Thursday, less than a year
after she won

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.520
the prestigious Goldman Environmental Prize.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:33.240
This particular murder
was particularly brazen.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.820
Someone thought that they
could murder a very, very

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.360
prominent person that
they would be able to

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.310
get away with that.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.630
This is a story that I had been following

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.060
since the actual murder in 2016.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.570
And as I followed it over the years,

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.160
the investigation evolved,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.850
it kept taking different twists and turns.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.460
There is no evidence
whatsoever that could link me

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.380
to the killing of Berta.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.800
Berta Cáceres was born into
a very political family,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.490
a family of activists, really.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.950
Her mother was one of the first
female mayors in Honduras.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.350
She was the mayor of a
town called La Esperanza.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.340
Berta, she was raised a Catholic.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.030
She was not raised in a
traditionally indigenous household.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.900
But by the time she reached her 20s,

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.770
she identified as a Lenca woman.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:42.190
That's the biggest indigenous
group, native to Honduras.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:59.810
She co-founded a human rights
organization called COPINH

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:03.500
that was basically for indigenous
communities in Honduras.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.560
And so by her 30s, she
was a national leader.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.490
She was someone who was
extremely passionate

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:14.490
about the rights of the
downtrodden, the marginalized,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.580
the indigenous in her country.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.300
She was also very amiable,
very pleasant, very friendly.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.230
She was good fun.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.890
But behind the laughter and the smiles,

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:26.690
you knew that she had a steely cool.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.100
Angry protestors at the doorsteps of

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.820
Honduras's Presidential Palace

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.750
want President Manuel Zelaya back.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.690
There was a coup in Honduras in 2009

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:53.790
and the government that took
over really put development

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:57.280
as one of the main
priorities for the country.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.570
And with that came this big building boom.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:06.140
Honduras does not produce
many natural resources

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.260
that can be used for energy.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.420
But one thing that it does
have is a lot of rivers.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.000
So you had a lot of hydroelectric dams

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.320
that suddenly were slated for areas

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.320
where there was very little development.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.730
One of these companies
that formed at this time

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.110
was called DESA and its sole purpose was

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:28.260
to develop the Agua Zarca
dam in Western Honduras.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.620
You had international
development banks funding it

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.150
along with private investment in Honduras.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.790
And Berta Cáceres heard
about this project,

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.220
knew that there was a Lenca
community that lived nearby,

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.070
and she really led an opposition movement.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.240
The residents worried
that the dam project would

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.960
flood out the areas that
were critical for them

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.060
to grow staples like corn and coffee.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.820
But even more than that, they argued that

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.680
the river itself was sacred

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.890
and that to dam it in this way

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:12.500
was a violation of Lenca tradition.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.380
No!

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.060
Residents began staging protests,

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.460
doing things like creating roadblocks.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.310
They would stack stones on the roads

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.930
so construction equipment
could not reach the work site.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.400
If you talk to DESA, the
hydro electric company,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.260
they will say that the majority of people

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:01.050
in the communities living around the river

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.660
supported the project.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:04.240
This is a point of contention.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:22.390
They argue that the community
was in support of the dam

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:26.190
although there were protests and marches

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.200
going on at the same time.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.090
No!

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.170
DESA decided this is a project

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:42.890
that it would move forward with

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:46.230
without consultation
with local communities.

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:50.290
And there has been just
a tremendous amount

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:51.590
of bloodshed and violence.

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:58.680
On July 15th, 2013,
there was a spontaneous

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.010
impromptu rally at the DESA work site.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:05.780
And this is a date that
everybody on both sides

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.010
of this conflict remembers very well.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.620
Tomas García, who was a COPINH protester,

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:18.710
was among a crowd who
entered the DESA work site.

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:22.040
And one of the guards, a security guard,

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:26.850
shot and killed Tomas and shot
and wounded his son, Alan.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.390
Right after the violence
happened, DESA's contractor,

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:43.090
Sinohydro pulled out of the project.

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:46.350
They abandoned the work site
and they never came back.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.450
DESA, instead of abandoning the project,

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:09.610
they actually redesigned it.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:14.290
So they moved the site of the
dam two kilometers up river,

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:15.860
and they also downsized it

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:18.550
to be what's called a
run of the river project.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:21.150
So there's less impact to the environment,

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:25.590
but as the protesters contended,
there's not zero impact.

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:29.750
So they had their first
public protest against the dam

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:34.130
in its new location in November of 2015.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:38.090
And that's when we think
that plans to murder Berta

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:41.340
really began to materialize.

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.690
She would tell a lot of her friends

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:49.940
that she would be getting
threats from people at DESA.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:52.510
You better watch out, watch your back.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.020
She reported cars
following her on the roads.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.870
You know, she would often
look behind her shoulder.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.520
She had people who would
help her with security.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:05.850
She was definitely scared.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.800
She knew she was at risk.

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:41.070
In 2015, she won the Goldman prize,

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:43.810
which is sometimes called the Green Nobel.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:45.390
It's really the biggest prize

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.340
an environmental activist can get.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.110
And it really kind of pushed her up

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:51.950
into a different level of notoriety.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:14.410
With hindsight, maybe we could
say that the Goldman prize

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:15.610
put her at greater risk.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.150
It's something that we
need to grapple with this

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.030
in the international
human rights community.

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:23.990
Whether we think that
it's a deterrent effect

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:26.290
to be able to raise someone's
profile in the media,

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:28.510
or if that puts them at greater risk.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:29.610
It's very hard to say.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:46.710
The prize also came with
$175,000, as a cash award.

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:50.990
And so Berta went back to
Honduras and a lot of that money,

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:52.590
she poured into COPINH,

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:56.770
into new projects and new
facilities in La Esperanza.

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:02.180
In March of 2016, she
had actually put together

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.620
a workshop in La Esperanza,

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:07.430
and one of the people who
was speaking at this workshop

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:09.420
was named Gustavo Castro.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:11.550
He's an environmentalist from Mexico.

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:15.400
He was planning on
staying at another house

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.370
with someone else in La Esperanza,

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:19.330
and he went out to visit Berta

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:23.230
and saw how isolated her house was.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.740
This house was located on
the outskirts of La Esperanza

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:32.240
on the far edge of town.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.740
And they started chatting and
she up inviting him to stay

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:37.070
in her guest bedroom.

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:04.810
Gustavo was in his room,
in the guest bedroom,

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:06.990
and Berta was down the hall.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:09.660
And Gustavo heard a noise in the kitchen.

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:25.410
Before he could even leave
the room to check things out,

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:26.660
he heard the bangs,

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:29.470
there were gunshots
coming from Berta's room.

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:50.900
The gunman apparently thought
that he was dead and left.

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.490
And after he heard the
gunman leave the house,

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:57.040
he got up and checked on Berta.

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:22.930
Gustavo searched around for a telephone,

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:24.460
found Berta's phone,

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:28.420
and just started calling
everybody on her contacts.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:33.080
When he eventually did contact
people, friends of Berta's,

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:35.530
they advised him not to call the police.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.890
I think it's safe to say
that oftentimes the police

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:42.050
were part of the problem,
not part of the solution.

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:44.030
There are terrible stories
of the police hierarchy

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:47.580
being involved in murder-for-hire schemes.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.310
If you called the police,

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:51.340
they may be as likely to shake you down

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:52.590
as they were to help you.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:59.050
So friends of Berta's helped
get him to another house.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:01.670
And the police, when they did arrive,

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:04.880
they found his suitcase
in the spare bedroom.

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:08.460
And so the police
identified him as a suspect,

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:12.640
but pretty much everyone
who was close to Berta

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:16.060
simply assumed that
this had something to do

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.390
with her opposition to the dam project.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:02.600
It was a complete shock.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.840
It was a shock, but at the same time,

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:06.570
it wasn't a surprise if that makes sense.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:09.320
Because of the people that
she was fighting against.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:22.580
The fact that Berta was killed,

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:25.650
is very clear to us in the
human rights community that

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.970
if Berta could be killed with impunity,

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:29.310
any of us could be killed.

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:43.420
I think everyone saw this as a test.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:48.040
Would Honduras be up to the challenge

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:51.250
of investigating this
murder in a professional way

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:54.240
and taking it wherever it might lead?

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:57.900
Or would this notion of
impunity carry the day?

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:06.450
From the beginning, it
seemed like the police

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:11.100
were wanting to classify
this as a crime of passion.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:14.040
So they were looking at Gustavo Castro

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:15.390
as a person of interest.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:17.280
He had been in the house with her.

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:20.350
He's been shot twice.

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:23.390
The Mexican Ambassador tried
to get him out of Honduras.

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:26.340
He's now been taken again
back to La Esperanza

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:28.790
where he was shot and
she was assassinated.

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:44.980
I thought this was a classic
way to try and deflect

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:48.080
from the real perpetrators,

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:51.060
particularly when a woman is the victim

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:54.030
that they think that a crime of passion

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:55.930
is something that people will swallow.

00:17:06.330 --> 00:17:08.650
Berta's daughters were really instrumental

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:11.340
in trying to push the police into looking

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.550
at the company DESA.

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.390
They reached out to other
activists around the world

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:19.690
for people to spread the
word about what had happened.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:21.610
I'm calling on the Honduran government

00:17:21.610 --> 00:17:24.970
to conduct a thorough and
transparent investigation

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:27.850
into the tragic murder of Berta Cáseres.

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:30.210
It doesn't look like they're
going to get justice,

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.890
or proper investigations
unless outside protest

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:36.650
really puts pressure on
the Honduran government.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:46.910
I think a lot of media
attention all over the world,

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.710
put pressure on the Honduran
state to act responsibly

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:51.990
and to do a genuine investigation.

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:28.230
Gustavo was finally allowed to leave

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:31.200
and essentially his name was cleared.

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.480
And the focus of the investigation

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:35.980
now turned directly towards DESA

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:43.400
The police began analyzing phone data.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:45.720
So they looked at the cell phone towers

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:49.160
closest to Berta's house
and found the phones

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.760
that were active in the area

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:52.900
at the time of the murder.

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:55.320
And so they began putting
the pieces together.

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:05.580
On May 2nd, 2016, police
launched a series of raids

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:06.620
early in the morning,

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:09.310
and they arrested several people that they

00:19:09.310 --> 00:19:11.900
alleged were involved in the murder.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:14.930
And these included alleged hitmen,

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:18.770
Mariano Díaz, who was
accused of being a middleman,

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:21.250
and Douglas Bustillo, who was

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.710
a former security chief for DESA.

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.540
Really the most important
thing that came of those raids

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:30.790
was what the police were able to collect.

00:19:30.790 --> 00:19:33.890
The cell phones of the
people who were arrested.

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:37.460
They were able to retrieve
thousands and thousands

00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:40.610
of text messages that became very critical

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:44.360
to reconstruct what
happened in this murder.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:49.590
I received a call from a colleague

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:54.590
and I was asked to participate
in the investigation.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:56.940
I was told that the family

00:19:56.940 --> 00:20:00.780
didn't trust Honduran authorities,

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:02.650
and that they wanted an independent

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:04.370
investigation of the murder.

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:22.450
The public prosecutor had access to really

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:25.640
a treasure trove of digital data.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:29.240
And they gave us access
to a small fraction.

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:33.590
And we used that to
identify the parameters

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:34.960
of the murder plot.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:42.750
So Berta was killed on March 2nd, 2016.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:45.270
On February 6th, a month before her death,

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:46.730
we can see in the texts,

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:49.810
probably the first
attempt on Berta's life.

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:55.430
This man named Henrry Hernandez is sent

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:58.650
to the town of La
Esperanza, where Berta was.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:02.370
And he's supposed to be
met there by Mariano Díaz,

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:04.340
an active member of the military.

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:09.340
We know that because Mariano
Díaz's phone had been tapped

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:10.970
because he was being investigated

00:21:10.970 --> 00:21:13.710
for involvement in drug trafficking

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:17.240
and the transcripts of
those wiretaps demonstrate

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:20.030
that Mariano Díaz is
gonna get Hernandez a gun.

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:24.650
At the same time, Mariano
Díaz is in conversation with

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:29.010
Douglas Bustillo, former
Head of Security of DESA.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:34.000
Douglas Bustillo is asking
Mariano Díaz questions like,

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:36.080
have you gotten on the bus?

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:36.980
Are you there yet?

00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:46.480
Later Douglas Bustillo
texts David Castillo,

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:48.810
the president of the company, DESA,

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:51.350
and tells him mission aborted.

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:58.530
That mission was abandoned
because Berta was not alone.

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:02.060
Her family was visiting her
and so there wasn't really

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:05.630
an opportunity to execute the plan.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:12.520
Additionally, when the
investigators were looking into

00:22:12.520 --> 00:22:16.370
Douglas Bustillo's phone, they
found images of Berta on it

00:22:16.370 --> 00:22:20.340
and also pictures that
he'd taken of her house.

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:23.980
So they believe he was
essentially stalking Berta.

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:28.720
And then later around
the time of the murder,

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:30.880
they saw that Douglas Bustillo

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:33.050
was in contact with the hitmen.

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:37.580
So the investigators were
able to make that link

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:39.800
from the hitmen, through the middleman,

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:43.540
through Douglas Bustillo,
to the executives at DESA

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:46.970
to piece together what they describe as

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:51.290
this long running corporate
conspiracy to try to kill Berta.

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:57.530
And so the investigation
really led to the CEO of DESA,

00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:58.780
David Castillo.

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:16.320
So David Castillo was taken
into custody in March of 2018.

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:20.560
I visited Castillo early in 2020,

00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:23.320
and he was being held at a military prison

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.580
just outside of Tegucigalpa.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:29.500
And I was able to sit
down with him in his cell

00:23:29.500 --> 00:23:33.160
and conduct a couple
of interviews with him.

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:50.540
David Castillo becomes involved in DESA

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:54.420
when he is still a member of the military.

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:59.340
He's integral to establishing,
for founding DESA.

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:03.670
And we were particularly
interested in his background

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:06.920
as a military intelligence officer.

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:10.250
So David essentially says

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:11.550
that the prosecutors have

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:14.560
totally misinterpreted these texts.

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:17.560
That for example, whenever
there was a mission

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:19.420
that was aborted, that they talk about it

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:22.320
being a mission to kill Berta.

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:25.100
He says, no that it was
something completely different

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:29.220
and they've twisted the
meaning to implicate him.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:46.570
So one of David's main points

00:24:46.570 --> 00:24:51.570
is he insists that he and Berta
we're actually good friends.

00:25:10.690 --> 00:25:15.200
He has text messages that
his defense has pulled

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:18.260
from his phone between him and Berta.

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:21.550
And many of these messages
do indicate a friendship.

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:50.020
She was very suspicious of
David Castillo and his motives.

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:56.130
That doesn't mean that she
did not regard it as strategic

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:58.970
to have a relationship with him.

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:14.630
What we can also see is
that he is contacting Berta,

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:18.060
having conversations with her,

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:20.620
obtaining information
about her whereabouts

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:25.320
and her activities, and then
funneling that information

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:28.070
to the company executives and employees.

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:33.620
How I interpret this
dynamic is quite typical

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:36.160
of a military intelligence officer.

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:39.700
It's using Berta as human intelligence

00:26:39.700 --> 00:26:43.300
to inform how the company
wants to handle opposition

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:44.880
to the dam project.

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:11.250
I think it's fair to say that in Honduras,

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:13.910
people have been able
to get away with murder.

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:16.880
The often quoted statistic is that

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:21.880
only about 3% of murders
in Honduras were solved.

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:27.170
So I think if you had asked
people on that terrible morning,

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:29.170
when Berta Cáceres was murdered,

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:33.010
if they thought that people
would be held to account

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:35.880
for this crime, I think most
people would have said no.

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:51.180
Now the question of course is,

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:56.030
is that the ultimate chain of
command for the murder or not?

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:15.810
So this investigation started
with the hitmen essentially,

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:17.530
the people who pulled the trigger.

00:28:17.530 --> 00:28:22.530
It went to David Castillo, the
CEO and that executive level.

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:26.590
And now the Cáceres
family is sort of testing

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:30.830
this concept to see how
far you can extend this.

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:33.390
So now they're going after actually

00:28:33.390 --> 00:28:36.840
the investors in the dam project.

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:59.490
I think it's far too easy for investors

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:03.190
to separate themselves from
the impacts of their money,

00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:05.780
which is usually in the global south.

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:07.750
It's their money, which is facilitating

00:29:07.750 --> 00:29:10.500
and being used to carry out the projects,

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:12.840
which are linked to so many
of these cases of attacks

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:14.360
and killings of activists.

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:21.520
Berta's supporters and her
family came up with a slogan

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:24.420
almost immediately after she was murdered.

00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:29.420
And it's translated, Berta
wasn't killed, she multiplied.

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:49.750
That's the message that
they take from this,

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:53.650
that even though her killers
tried to silence her,

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:55.470
her voice is louder now

00:30:55.470 --> 00:30:57.520
than it had ever been during her life.

